      },
      "lineNbr": 196,
      "author": {
        "id": 1044551
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-03-25T14:29:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "the reason I put the kobject in the mount_info was because I could find no other way of passing the mount_info pointer to the incfs_get_##name function, and this seems to match what ext4 does (https://elixir.bootlin.com/linux/v5.12-rc4/source/fs/ext4/ext4.h#L1490)\nHowever, I notice ext uses a struct completion s_kobj_unregister which perhaps fixes lifespan issues?",
      "parentUuid": "f8085e6d_1bc9c6ae",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 196,
        "startChar": 16,
        "endLine": 196,
        "endChar": 29
      },
      "revId": "df2dd3d027f6ba792540fb9be26b414b4ef93cd6",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "79598aea_0ea0c598",
        "filename": "fs/incfs/data_mgmt.h",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 196,
      "author": {
        "id": 1125653
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-03-25T14:41:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\"fixes\" is a kind term for that hack, but yes, that does help out here by making the kobject removal synchronous so that you \"know\" it is cleaned up.\n\nBut I really don\u0027t like that if at all possible.  Why not just put the kobject and statistics in a separate object with a separate lifecycle and have a pointer to that structure here?  That would clean up the lifetime rules a lot and make it more obvious what they are.",
      "parentUuid": "f4622584_dd2e5912",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 196,
